¶
'''Admete''' (ad-mē′tē), ''n.'' [NL., < Gr. ἀδμητος, fem. ἀδμήτη, untamed, unbroken, poet. form of ἀδάματος = E. ''untamed''. Cf. ''adamant''.] The typical genus of gastropods of the family ''Admetidæ''. {{smaller|''A. viridula'' is a small whitish species, half an inch long, found on the Atlantic coast of North America from Cape Cod northward.}}

'''admetid''' (ad-mē′tid), ''n.'' A gastropod of the family ''Admetidæ''.

'''Admetidæ''' (ad-met′i-dē), ''n. pl.'' [NL., < ''Admete'' + ''-idæ''.] A family of toxoglossate pectinibranchiate gastropods, typified by the genus ''Admete''. {{smaller|The family is closely related to the ''Cancellariidæ'', but the species affect colder waters. ''Admete viridula'' is a common northern form. The members of this family have a rounded head, filiform tentacles, eyes on minute tubercles external to the tentacles, and a characteristic dentition of the odontophore; the shell has an ovate aperture, with an obliquely truncated plicate columella and a trenchant outer lip.}}

'''adminicle''' (ad-min′i-kl), ''n.'' [< L. ''adminiculum'', help, support, prop, lit. that on which the hand may rest, < ''ad'', to, + ''manus'', hand, + double dim. suffix ''-culum''.] 1. That which gives aid or support; an auxiliary. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|The senate of five hundred . . . was a permanent adjunct and ''adminicle'' of the public assembly.
{{right|''Grote'', Greece, III. 99.}}}}
2. In ''law'', supporting or corroboratory proof. {{smaller|Specifically, in ''Scots'' and ''French law'', whatever aids in proving the tenor of a lost deed; any deed or scroll which tends to establish the existence of the deed in question, or to make known its terms.}}

3. In ''med.'', any aid to the action of a remedy. —4. ''pl.'' In ''entom.'' See ''adminiculum'', 2.

'''adminicula''', ''n.'' Plural of ''adminiculum''.

readings.c3s1:
¶
'''adminicular, adminiculary''' (ad-mi-nik′ụ̄-lär, -lạ̄-ri), ''a.'' [< L. ''adminiculum'', help: see ''adminicle.''] Supplying help; helpful; corroborative.
{{smaller block|The humanity of Christ is not set before us in the New Testament as sustaining merely a conditional or ''adminicular'' relation to a work whose intrinsic and essential value comes from another source. {{right|''Prog. Orthodoxy'', p. 20.}}}}
{{smaller block|The several structural arrangements ''adminicular'' to the integrity of the whole are thus co-ordinated.
{{right|''H. Spencer'', Prin. of Psychol.}}}}

{{smaller|'''Adminicular evidence''', in ''law'', explanatory or completing evidence.}}

'''adminiculate''' (ad-mi-nik′ụ̄-lāt), ''v. i.'' or ''t.'' [< L. ''adminiculatus'', pp. of ''adminiculare'', help, prop, < ''adminiculum'': see ''adminicle.''] To give adminicular evidence; testify in corroboration of. [Rare.]

'''adminiculator†''' (ad-mi-nik′ụ̄-lā-tor), ''n.'' [L., < ''adminiculare'': see ''adminiculate.''] An assistant; specifically, an advocate for the poor.

'''adminiculum''' (ad-mi-nik′ụ̄-lum), ''n.''; pl. ''adminicula'' (-lä). [L., a prop: see ''adminicle.''] 1. An aid or help; an adminicle.
{{smaller block|Of other ''adminicula'', or aids to induction, only the titles are given by Bacon, and it would be hazardous to conjecture as to their significance.
{{right|''R. Adamson'', Encyc. Brit., XIV. 792.}}}}
2. ''pl.'' In ''entom.'', Kirby’s name for the short spines on the abdominal segments of certain insects, pupæ or grubs, whereby they make their way through any substance in which they burrow. Also called ''adminicles.'' ''N. E. D.''

readings.c3s3:
{{smaller block|<poem>Swear by the duty that you owe to Heaven . . .
To keep the oath that we ''administer.''</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Rich. II., i. 3.}}}}
5. In ''law'', to manage or dispose of, as the estate of a deceased person, in the capacity either of executor or administrator. See ''administration'', 9. {{smaller|='''Syn.''' 1. To control, preside over.—2 and 3. ''Administer'', ''Minister'', distribute, give out, deal out. In the sense of supplying, dispensing, ''minister'' is now used principally of things spiritual: as, to ''minister'' comfort, consolation, or relief; while ''administer'' is used of things both spiritual and material: as, to ''administer'' food, medicine, reproof, justice.}}
{{smaller block|He asserted that . . . a noxious drug had been ''administered'' to him in a dish of porridge.
{{right|''Macaulay'', Hist. Eng., xv.}}
The greatest delight which the fields and woods ''minister'' is the suggestion of an occult relation between man and the vegetable.
{{right|''Emerson'', Nature.}}}}

II. ''intrans.'' 1. To contribute assistance; bring aid or supplies; add something: with ''to'': as, to ''administer to'' the necessities of the poor.
{{smaller block|There is a fountain rising in the upper part of my garden, which . . . ''administers to'' the pleasure as well as the plenty of the place.
{{right|''Spectator'', No. 447.}}}}
2. To perform the office of administrator: with ''upon'': as, A ''administers upon'' the estate of B. {{smaller|='''Syn.''' ''Administer to'', ''Minister to''. ''Minister to'' is now preferable to ''administer to'' in such connections as to ''minister to'' one’s needs, to ''minister to'' the necessities of the poor, to ''minister to'' the pleasure of the assembly. ''Administer to'' in such connections is archaic.}}

'''administer†''' (ad-min′is-tėr), ''n.'' [L.: see the verb.] One who administers; a minister or an administrator.
{{smaller block|You have shewed yourself a good ''administer'' of the revenue.
{{right|''Bacon'', Speech to Sir J. Denham.}}}}
